\subsection{Sound Reset}

Clear all state and store the voice ROM pointer.

<<sound reset implementation>>=
void sound_reset(SoundState& sound, const uint8_t* voice_rom, uint32_t size)
{
    sound.speech = S14001AState{};
    sound.timer = Exidy6840State{};
    sound.voice_rom = voice_rom;
    sound.voice_rom_size = size;
}
@

\subsection{S14001A Write}

Port 0x44 write has a mode field in bits 7--6:

\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{00}: Load word address from bits 5--0 and trigger playback.
  In the stub, we set the word address but immediately clear busy (since
  we are not actually playing audio).
\item \texttt{01}: Set volume (bits 5--3) and clock divisor (bits 2--0).
  Divisor is [[16 - (data \& 0x07)]].
\item \texttt{10}, \texttt{11}: Unused modes, ignored.
\end{itemize}

<<s14001a write implementation>>=
void s14001a_write(SoundState& sound, uint8_t data)
{
    uint8_t mode = (data @>> 6) & 0x03;
    switch (mode) {
    case 0:
        // Trigger playback of word at address (bits 5-0)
        sound.speech.word_addr = data & 0x3F;
        // Stub: set busy briefly. Since we have no timing, clear it
        // immediately.  If games spin-wait too tightly, we may need
        // a cycle counter here.
        sound.speech.busy = false;
        break;
    case 1:
        // Volume and clock divisor
        sound.speech.volume = (data @>> 3) & 0x07;
        sound.speech.clock_div = 16 - (data & 0x07);
        break;
    default:
        // Modes 2 and 3 are unused
        break;
    }
}
@

\subsection{S14001A Read}

Port 0x44 read returns the busy status in bit 6.  When busy, bit 6 is
clear (0x00).  When ready (not busy), bit 6 is set (0x40).  All other
bits read as 0.

<<s14001a read implementation>>=
uint8_t s14001a_read(const SoundState& sound)
{
    // Bit 6: 0 = busy, 0x40 = ready
    return sound.speech.busy ? 0x00 : 0x40;
}
@

\subsection{Exidy 6840 Timer Write}

Ports 0x40--0x43, 0x45, 0x47 map to the 6840 timer registers.  Port
addresses are decoded as offsets into the 8-register file.  The stub
simply stores the written value.

The offset mapping from I/O port to register index:
\begin{itemize}
\item Port 0x40 $\rightarrow$ offset 0
\item Port 0x41 $\rightarrow$ offset 1
\item Port 0x42 $\rightarrow$ offset 2
\item Port 0x43 $\rightarrow$ offset 3
\item Port 0x45 $\rightarrow$ offset 5
\item Port 0x47 $\rightarrow$ offset 7
\end{itemize}

<<exidy6840 write implementation>>=
void exidy6840_write(SoundState& sound, uint8_t port, uint8_t data)
{
    uint8_t offset = port - 0x40;
    if (offset < 8) {
        sound.timer.regs[offset] = data;
    }
}
@

\subsection{Exidy 6840 Timer Read}

Returns the stored register value.  On real hardware, some registers
(like the status register) have more complex read behavior, but the
stub is sufficient for the game to proceed past initialization.

<<exidy6840 read implementation>>=
uint8_t exidy6840_read(const SoundState& sound, uint8_t port)
{
    uint8_t offset = port - 0x40;
    if (offset < 8) {
        return sound.timer.regs[offset];
    }
    return 0xFF;
}
@

\subsection{SFX Control Latch Write}

Port 0x46 write sets the sound effect control latch.  Bits 7--6 select
the function.  The stub stores the value for observability.

<<sfx latch write implementation>>=
void sfx_latch_write(SoundState& sound, uint8_t data)
{
    sound.timer.sfx_latch = data;
}
@

